{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "strict": true,
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true
  },
  "include": ["src"],
  /*
   * The files outside this program, and the reason is a deliberate trade rather
   * than an oversight.
   *
   * Two files now, for one reason. `verify/ppg-check.ts` imports
   * `client/src/lib/averages.ts` so that it runs the SHIPPED averaging module over
   * the real 253,509 rows; `verify/columns-check.ts` imports
   * `client/src/lib/playerColumns.ts` so that it asks the SHIPPED picker what it
   * would offer. A verification that reimplements the rule it is checking proves
   * nothing — that is the failure the Working Agreement names — so importing the
   * real module is the point of both.
   *
   * `rootDir` is `./src`, so pulling a file from `../client` into the program raises
   * TS6059 on it and on everything it imports. Removing `rootDir` would re-root the
   * emit at the repo root and turn `dist/index.js` into `dist/server/src/index.js`,
   * breaking `npm start`; project references would be a bigger change than the
   * script is worth.
   *
   * Cost, stated because it is real: **these two files are not typechecked**. Both
   * are read-only scripts, run by hand, and `tsx` executes them correctly —
   * verified. Two is the point at which this stops being a one-off; if a third
   * arrives, the right fix is a shared package rather than a wider `rootDir`.
   */
  "exclude": ["src/verify/ppg-check.ts", "src/verify/columns-check.ts"]
}
